      "commitid": "f65ab3f50ca81475b1a4d84262932cde174a3749",
      "message": "fix(tests): declare PyYAML, which the suite had been borrowing\n\nThe tests read the workflow files, so several of them `import yaml`. Nothing in\npyproject.toml asked for PyYAML. It arrived through mutmut -> libcst, and it had\nworked for long enough that nobody had reason to look.\n\nOn 2026-09-25 libcst's requirement turned out to be conditional on the\ninterpreter: `pyyaml>=5.2` on most versions, and `pyyaml-ft>=8.0.0` — the\nfree-threading fork, which installs no module called `yaml` — on Python 3.13.\nSo the import resolved on 3.11, 3.12 and 3.14 and not on 3.13. One matrix entry\nout of four, for a package this repository had never mentioned.\n\nA borrowed dependency works until its lender changes its mind, and the lender is\nunder no obligation to say so.\n\nDeclared here rather than worked around: the tests genuinely need to parse YAML,\nand a test that parses a workflow file by regular expression to avoid an import\nis a worse test.\n\nThe other half of this is tests/test_declared_imports.py, which compares what\nthe suite imports against what the project declares, resolving each module to\nthe distribution that really provides it — `yaml` comes from PyYAML — so there\nis no name map to keep in step with anything.\n\nThe rule it enforces is not \"never import what you do not declare\", because\nthere is a legitimate version of that. apkradar reaches into `loguru` on\npurpose: it belongs to androguard, and silencing androguard's logger means\ntouching it. That is fine, and the test that does it says\n`pytest.importorskip(\"loguru\")` first, so it skips rather than breaks if loguru\never goes away. The rule is: what you do not declare, the suite has to survive\nlosing. `import yaml` at module level did not — it stopped collection, and took\n388 unrelated tests with it.\n\nChecked by mutation rather than by assertion alone. Removing the new pyyaml line\nturns the guard red on `yaml`; replacing apkradar's importorskip with a plain\n`import loguru` turns it red on `loguru`; both go green again on restore.",
      "timestamp": "2026-09-25T23:53:46Z",
